🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗩𝗖 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗰𝗸 𝗶𝗻 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You created a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC), but its status remains Pending. Your application cannot start because storage is unavailable. What will you check?”

In production, I wouldn't delete and recreate the PVC immediately. First, I'd identify why Kubernetes cannot provision or bind the requested storage.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check the PVC status

kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pvc <pvc-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events section for provisioning or binding errors.

2️⃣ Verify the StorageClass

kubectl get storageclass
kubectl describe storageclass <storageclass-name>

Confirm that the requested StorageClass exists and supports the required configuration.

3️⃣ Check available PersistentVolumes

kubectl get pv

For static provisioning, verify that a suitable PV exists and matches the PVC's storage capacity, access modes and StorageClass.

4️⃣ Check dynamic provisioning

If using dynamic provisioning, investigate the CSI provisioner, storage backend, permissions and provisioner logs.

5️⃣ Review access modes and capacity

Check whether the requested storage size and access modes are supported by the storage solution.

6️⃣ Check volume binding and scheduling

A PVC can remain Pending when its StorageClass uses "WaitForFirstConsumer", because provisioning or binding may wait for Pod scheduling constraints to be evaluated.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

PVC Pending ≠ Always a storage failure.

The cause could be a missing StorageClass, unavailable PV, provisioning failure, unsupported access mode or scheduling constraint.
